// A Radeon's video BIOS ROM: read from the card, held to be one, and its
// tables read. The ROM holds what differs from board to board: which
// connectors there are and what they hang on, the clocks, and the small
// programs that set a mode's pixel clock and transmitter (atom.mlx runs
// those). Reading it happens at every boot; nothing is run from it here.
// Built for the host too (tests/473_gpu_rom_runtime.mlx, on ROMs the test
// makes): the card is reached through `Bus`.
//
// THE WAYS TO THE ROM. Linux's amdgpu tries seven in this order
// (amdgpu_bios.c, amdgpu_get_bios): the ACPI method ATRM (a notebook's
// second card); the ACPI table VFCT; the start of the card's memory (a
// board's own graphics); the PCI expansion ROM (pci_map_rom); the card's
// ROM registers (vi.c, vi_read_bios_from_rom); the expansion ROM with the
// card's ROM straps and VGA switched (vi_read_disabled_bios); and what
// the platform kept (a UEFI firmware's copy, by way of Linux's EFI stub).
// Three are built, tried in Linux's order, the first that gives a ROM
// that holds wins:
// 1. VFCT (`fromVfct`). The card's UEFI driver leaves a copy of the ROM
//    in an ACPI table of that name; it is not promised (a machine started
//    by a BIOS has none), but where it is, it is the firmware's own copy
//    and reading it touches nothing of the card: memory is read, no more.
// 2. The PCI expansion ROM (`fromExpansion`): the way every PCI device's
//    ROM is read, and the way the machine's firmware read this one to
//    find the card's driver. It needs the ROM's address to be assigned
//    (the firmware may have taken it away again; none is made up here,
//    since a free place in the bridge's window is not known), and to be
//    one the firmware meant: an address a firmware only left behind (all
//    ones after it learned the size, or a place it has since given to
//    something else) must not be switched on, so the ROM is switched on
//    only where its bytes lie wholly inside the memory window of the
//    bridge the card sits behind and on none of the card's other
//    memories (`mayAnswer`).
//    WRITTEN: the card's PCI configuration word 0x30 (the ROM's address
//    and its enable bit, PCI_ROM_ADDRESS), four times: all ones in the
//    address bits with the enable bit clear, to learn the ROM's size;
//    what it held; then, only when the address is one the ROM may be
//    switched on at, what it held with the enable bit set, while the
//    bytes are read; and what it held. Nothing of the card's registers.
// 3. The card's ROM registers (`fromRegisters`): the system management
//    controller's index and data pair (SMC_IND_INDEX_11 and SMC_IND_DATA_
//    11), through which ROM_INDEX is set to 0 and ROM_DATA read again and
//    again, a long of the ROM each time.
//    WRITTEN: SMC_IND_INDEX_11 (which register the pair shows) and,
//    through the pair, ROM_INDEX (where in the ROM the next read is);
//    both are read first and put back as they were.
// Not built: ATRM and the start of the card's memory (other machines);
// the ROM with its straps and the VGA registers switched (that writes the
// display's registers); the 0xC0000 shadow Linux's pci_map_rom takes
// for the boot display (a machine started by UEFI has none); the
// platform's copy (the EFI loader would have to pass the firmware's ROM
// image on, as it passes the EDID: the next way to add should these
// three give nothing on a machine).
//
// NOTHING OF THIS DEPENDS ON WHERE THE CARD SITS OR WHAT IT SHOWS. The
// card is named by its place on the PCI bus as the kernel found it
// (`Place`), whichever slot that is: the VFCT's image is the one with
// that place and the card's numbers (a table may hold another adapter's
// too), the expansion ROM and the ROM registers are the card's own. A
// card that is not the firmware's display, or that the firmware has not
// started at all, has its ROM read the same way (a firmware that did not
// run the card's driver leaves no VFCT image for it and perhaps no
// address for its ROM: then the ROM registers give it, when the card's
// registers can be reached); no setting of the firmware's is needed.
// `read` goes through the ways.
//
// WHY READING CANNOT DISTURB THE PICTURE. The display controller shows
// the picture from the card's own memory by its own registers; none of
// them is written or even read here. Word 0x30 of the PCI configuration
// is the ROM's address decoder alone. One caution is PCI's (Linux says it
// in drivers/pci/rom.c): a device may use one decoder for its ROM and
// another of its memories, so that while the ROM is switched on its
// registers or memory do not answer: so between switching it on and off
// nothing else of the card is touched and the log is not written (the
// console draws into the card's memory); the kernel reads the ROM while
// it runs with its interrupts off, before the other processors are
// started and before any program runs (main.mlx probes the GPU there),
// so nothing else can touch the card or anything else meanwhile. The
// SMC's pair and the ROM's index are the management controller's; Linux
// reads the ROM through them on a running card with the firmware's
// picture up (amdgpu_get_bios runs before any mode is set).
//
// WHAT HOLDS A ROM. As Linux's amdgpu does (check_atom_bios, amdgpu_read_
// bios_from_rom, amdgpu_atom_parse): the first bytes 55 AA, AMD's mark
// " 761295520" at 0x30, a length (the byte at 2, in blocks of 512) that
// what was read holds, a header at the place the word at 0x48 names that
// says "ATOM". And one thing more, which amdgpu does not ask: that the
// bytes add up to 0 in eight bits, as a PCI ROM's do (Linux holds a video
// ROM to that in arch/x86/kernel/probe_roms.c, romchecksum). A ROM with
// the marks whose bytes do not add up is kept to be looked at and handed
// over, and none of its tables is run.
//
// THE TABLES READ, as Linux's display core reads them for this card
// (dc/bios/bios_parser.c; the older formats of atombios.h): the firmware
// information of format 2.2 (get_firmware_info_v2_2: the pixel clock
// PLL's reference and limits, the display engine's clock, the
// DisplayPort reference), and the object table: each display path's
// connector, its encoder (a connector's first source: bios_parser_get_
// src_obj) and by that its transmitter (translate_encoder_to_transmitter
// in dc/link/link_factory.c), which on this controller is also the
// number of the DIG block the link goes out by (dce112_resource.c,
// map_transmitter_id_to_phy_instance); the connector's hot plug pin and
// DDC line as the ROM numbers them (get_hpd_record, the I2C record). The
// structure layouts are atombios.h's and ObjectID.h's of Linux 6.6, each
// offset below named after its field and held against those files by
// tools/check_gpu_registers.py.
//
// The log lines ("gpu: video BIOS ...", "gpu: table ...", "gpu: firmware
// information ...", "gpu: connector N: ...") are said by `sayRom`,
// `sayTables`, `sayFirmware` and `sayBoard`.
const atom = @import("./atom.mlx")
const scanout = @import("./gpu_display.mlx")

// A ROM image's most bytes: 255 blocks of 512.
pub const ROOM: usize = 255 * 512

// The PCI configuration's ROM word (include/uapi/linux/pci_regs.h).
pub const PCI_COMMAND: usize = 0x04
pub const PCI_COMMAND_MEMORY: usize = 0x2
pub const PCI_ROM_ADDRESS: usize = 0x30
pub const PCI_ROM_ADDRESS_ENABLE: usize = 0x01
pub const PCI_ROM_ADDRESS_MASK: usize = 0xFFFFF800
// A bridge's memory window: its first and last MiB in the two words at
// 0x20 and 0x22, their low four bits not address bits.
pub const PCI_MEMORY_BASE: usize = 0x20
pub const PCI_MEMORY_RANGE_MASK: usize = 0xFFFFFFF0

// The management controller's index and data pair and the two ROM
// registers behind it (asic_reg/smu/smu_7_1_1_d.h, which vi.c reads the
// ROM by).
pub const SMC_IND_INDEX_11: usize = 0x1AC
pub const SMC_IND_DATA_11: usize = 0x1AD
pub const ROM_INDEX: usize = 0xC0600010
pub const ROM_DATA: usize = 0xC0600014

// The image's fixed places (atomfirmware.h's atombios_image_offset, with
// atom.h's place of AMD's mark; the name strings as atom.c reads them).
pub const OFFSET_TO_ATOM_ROM_IMAGE_SIZE: usize = 0x02
pub const OFFSET_TO_GET_ATOMBIOS_NUMBER_OF_STRINGS: usize = 0x2F
pub const OFFSET_TO_ATOM_ROM_HEADER_POINTER: usize = 0x48
pub const OFFSET_TO_VBIOS_DATE: usize = 0x50
pub const OFFSET_TO_GET_ATOMBIOS_STRING_START: usize = 0x6E
pub const OFFSET_TO_VBIOS_PART_NUMBER: usize = 0x80
pub const BIOS_STRING_LENGTH: usize = 43
pub const STRLEN_LONG: usize = 64

// The ACPI table VFCT (atombios.h, UEFI_ACPI_VFCT and VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER):
// where the first image's header lies, and in an image's header the
// card's place on the PCI bus, its numbers and the image's length; the
// image follows its header.
pub const UEFI_ACPI_VFCT__VBIOSImageOffset: usize = 0x34
pub const UEFI_ACPI_VFCT__size: usize = 0x4C
pub const VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER__PCIBus: usize = 0
pub const VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER__PCIDevice: usize = 4
pub const VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER__PCIFunction: usize = 8
pub const VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER__VendorID: usize = 12
pub const VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER__DeviceID: usize = 14
pub const VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER__ImageLength: usize = 24
pub const VFCT_IMAGE_HEADER__size: usize = 28

// The command tables' places in the ROM's list (atombios.h, ATOM_MASTER_
// LIST_OF_COMMAND_TABLES in its order), those a mode set runs and the one
// that is never run.
pub const Command = struct {
    pub const ASIC_Init: usize = 0
    pub const DIGxEncoderControl: usize = 4
    pub const EnableCRTCMemReq: usize = 6
    pub const SetPixelClock: usize = 12
    pub const EnableDispPowerGating: usize = 13
    pub const AdjustDisplayPll: usize = 17
    pub const BlankCRTC: usize = 34
    pub const EnableCRTC: usize = 35
    pub const SelectCRTC_Source: usize = 42
    pub const SetDCEClock: usize = 46
    pub const SetCRTC_UsingDTDTiming: usize = 49
    pub const EnableSpreadSpectrumOnPPLL: usize = 65
    pub const DIG1TransmitterControl: usize = 76
}

// The data tables' places in the ROM's list (ATOM_MASTER_LIST_OF_DATA_
// TABLES).
pub const Data = struct {
    pub const FirmwareInfo: usize = 4
    pub const VRAM_UsageByFirmware: usize = 11
    pub const Object_Header: usize = 22
}

// What a mode set on this controller runs, from Linux's two ways of
// doing it: the display core's (dc/bios/command_table.c: the pixel clock
// with its adjustment and spread, the DIG encoder and the transmitter,
// the timing, the controller on and its memory requests, the power gate,
// the display engine's clock) and the older amdgpu's (atombios_crtc.c
// and atombios_encoders.c: the blanking and which controller an encoder
// shows beside those). DIG1TransmitterControl is the table Linux calls
// UNIPHYTransmitterControl.
const NEEDED = [_]usize{ Command.SetPixelClock, Command.DIG1TransmitterControl, Command.DIGxEncoderControl, Command.EnableCRTC, Command.BlankCRTC, Command.SetCRTC_UsingDTDTiming, Command.EnableDispPowerGating, Command.AdjustDisplayPll, Command.EnableSpreadSpectrumOnPPLL, Command.SetDCEClock, Command.SelectCRTC_Source, Command.EnableCRTCMemReq }
const NEEDED_NAMES = [_][]const u8{ "SetPixelClock", "DIG1TransmitterControl (the UNIPHY transmitter)", "DIGxEncoderControl", "EnableCRTC", "BlankCRTC", "SetCRTC_UsingDTDTiming", "EnableDispPowerGating", "AdjustDisplayPll", "EnableSpreadSpectrumOnPPLL", "SetDCEClock", "SelectCRTC_Source", "EnableCRTCMemReq" }

// The firmware information of format 2.2 (ATOM_FIRMWARE_INFO_V2_2): the
// fields read, clocks in units of 10 kHz.
pub const ATOM_FIRMWARE_INFO_V2_2__ulFirmwareRevision: usize = 4
pub const ATOM_FIRMWARE_INFO_V2_2__ulMaxPixelClockPLL_Output: usize = 32
